  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Solve  $6{x}^{2}-7<5{x}^{2}+9.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}-7 & <5{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2} & <9+7 \\\\ {x}^{2} & <16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <16 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{16} \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have $-4<x<4.$\n\nSo, the solution is $\\,x\\in (-4,4).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}<16$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-16<0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y={x}^{2}-16$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x=\\pm 4.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-16$ is positive, the parabola opens upward. Therefore, the solution to the given inequality ($y<0$) lies between the roots (but does not include them).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/48ffe19b490220651ddba5095ae192c49df2e36d1b203031784fd8c3f4aaa559.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-4,4).$",
